182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 48 0 R
/T (Overfitting or underfitting? There is no sign of overfitting: overfitting shows as training performance climbing while validation loss turns upward, and here validation loss never rises. The model is also not clearly underfitting, because accuracy is well above the 50% chance level. It is probably slightly under-trained, since the loss is still decreasing. Validation loss sits below training loss, which looks odd but has ordinary causes: Keras reports training loss as an average over the epoch \(while the weights are still improving\), and the validation set has only 15 rows, so it can be a little easier than the training data. Caution. The validation set is tiny, so each validation row is worth 6.7 percentage points and the 93.3% figure is coarse. The more reliable number is the held-out test accuracy of 80% \(20 of 25 correct\), which is lower than validation accuracy. The gap is an expected effect of small samples, not evidence of a bug.)
/E (Overfitting or underfitting? There is no sign of overfitting: overfitting shows as training performance climbing while validation loss turns upward, and here validation loss never rises. The model is also not clearly underfitting, because accuracy is well above the 50% chance level. It is probably slightly under-trained, since the loss is still decreasing. Validation loss sits below training loss, which looks odd but has ordinary causes: Keras reports training loss as an average over the epoch \(while the weights are still improving\), and the validation set has only 15 rows, so it can be a little easier than the training data. Caution. The validation set is tiny, so each validation row is worth 6.7 percentage points and the 93.3% figure is coarse. The more reliable number is the held-out test accuracy of 80% \(20 of 25 correct\), which is lower than validation accuracy. The gap is an expected effect of small samples, not evidence of a bug.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
