468 0 obj
<<
/Title (Overfitting or underfitting? There is no sign of overfitting: overfitting shows as training performance climbing while validation loss turns upward, and here validation loss never rises. The model is also not clearly underfitting, because accuracy is well above the 50% chance level. It is probably slightly under-trained, since the loss is still decreasing. Validation loss sits below training loss, which looks odd but has ordinary causes: Keras reports training loss as an average over the epoch \(while the weights are still improving\), and the validation set has only 15 rows, so it can be a little easier than the training data. Caution. The validation set is tiny, so each validation row is worth 6.7 percentage points and the 93.3% figure is coarse. The more reliable number is the held-out test accuracy of 80% \(20 of 25 correct\), which is lower than validation accuracy. The gap is an expected effect of small samples, not evidence of a bug.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 422 0 R
>>
endobj
469 0 obj
<<
/Title (Output: predictions on three new records)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 319 0 R
/Prev 423 0 R
/First 492 0 R
/Last 493 0 R
/Count -10
/Next 424 0 R
>>
endobj
470 0 obj
<<
/Title (Record 1 \(no failed logins, daytime, known device, 35 MB\) is Normal for both models; the network probability of 0.193 is well below 0.50. Record 2 \(7 failed logins, 1 a.m., new device, 250 MB\) is Attack for both, but the network probability of 0.675 is only moderately above 0.50, so it is less confident, even though this record looks suspicious on three of four features. Record 3 \(2 failed logins, 11 p.m., new device, 850 MB\) is Attack for both, with a probability of 0.900; the large transfer drives this. The two models agree on all three records, which raises confidence in the labels. Had they disagreed, or had a probability been near 0.50, the record should be sent for analyst review.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 424 0 R
>>
endobj
